  "result": "I've written the revised record to `record.jsonl`; it hasn't been through the gate yet. The gate refused the old one because the signal-head variable `d` wasn't tied to the pedestrian through any shared atom. Comparing the head's position with the pedestrian's position and heading doesn't count as a tie.\n\n**What changed (span `s4`):**\n- **The tie:** span `s4` now uses `dev.governs`. A head pertains to the crossing when it governs the intersection the pedestrian enters. I tied it to the intersection rather than the crosswalk because the `dev.governs` contract only allows travel regions, and a crosswalk isn't one.\n- **Same intersection in the obligation:** the obligation selects that intersection again using every condition from the applicability. These are: the pedestrian's footprint starts to overlap it at this observation, at least one lane enters it, and the pedestrian crosses every entering lane obliquely.\n- **Atoms claimed:** span `s4` now also claims `dev.governs`, `rel.occupies`, `st.intersection`, `st.lane`, `st.lane_enters` and `drv.motion.moving_across_carrier`.\n- **Facing check kept:** the position/heading comparison stays, now only to pick, among the heads governing the intersection, the one across the diagonal crossing.\n- **New disclosed reading:** \"devices pertaining to such crossing movements\" is read as the pedestrian heads that govern the entered intersection and that the pedestrian faces.\n\n**New scenarios:**\n- `branch_3` (compliant): no pedestrian head in the scene. The rules require a decided case with none of the quantified party present.\n- `branch_4` (compliant): the faced head shows a steady don't-walk but governs a different intersection. It differs from `forbid` only in that tie.\n\n**Other edits:**\n- **Parameter proposals:** I removed the old ones. `p.ped_signal_facing_cos` and `p.ped_signal_facing_reach` are now in the catalogue and are named as they are there.\n- **Unknown verdicts:** where it isn't observed whether a faced head governs the intersection, and that head shows a forbidding indication, the verdict is unknown.\n\n**Risk:** the gate may still reject this, because the `dev.governs` contract describes control of an intersection as \"control of the junction itself\". Whether a pedestrian head governs the junction is my reading, not something the contract says outright. The coder's witnesses will also need to record which intersection the head governs; they currently don't.",
